<p>Photography occupies an unusual position among the arts. It is mechanical in a way that painting is not: a camera records whatever is in front of it. That mechanical quality has made people argue, since photography's invention, about whether it qualifies as art at all. Unit 11 takes that question seriously.</p>
<p>The unit introduces three major movements in the history of art photography. Pictorialism borrowed techniques from painting (soft focus, dramatic lighting, sentimental subjects) to elevate photography to the status of fine art. Straight photography rejected pictorialism and sought excellence in photographic technique itself, emphasizing sharp focus, clear composition, and the authority of the unmanipulated image. Documentary photography used the camera to record social conditions, often with a political purpose.</p>
<p>The question running through the unit is the same one that has followed us since Unit 2: what makes something a work of art? In photography, the answer depends heavily on how the photographer uses the elements of the medium (framing, light, focus, timing) to transform raw reality into something with artistic form.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Learning Objectives</p>
<p>After completing this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Distinguish between pictorialism, straight photography, and documentary photography</li>
<li>Explain how framing, light, focus, and timing function as elements of photographic form</li>
<li>Define sentimentality and explain why this unit treats it as a weakness in photography</li>
<li>Describe the concept of the "decisive moment" in documentary photography</li>
<li>Apply descriptive and interpretive criticism to a photograph</li>
</ul>




